// runtime/builtin/tryable.tin
//
// The `tryable` trait - the contract the `try` keyword desugars
// against. Partial-bound shape:
//
//   trait tryable[V, C] =
//     fn is_err(this tryable[V, C])    bool = virtual
//     fn ok_value(this tryable[V, C])  V    = virtual
//     fn err_value(this tryable[V, C]) C    = virtual
//
// The container C carries a wildcard slot for the success type, e.g.
// Result[_, E] or Option[_]. That wildcard is impl-resolved per
// (concrete data, trait) pair: at the impl site, `_` unifies with
// the data's success type. At the keyword desugar's
// `return err_value()`, the wildcard means the propagated container's
// success slot is independent of the enclosing function's success
// type - only the error/failure shape (E in Result, the unit of None
// in Option) has to be compatible.
//
// Methods are not contractually partial. Impls that have a structure
// where ok_value/err_value only make sense in one branch (Result,
// Option) panic in the wrong branch by their own choice; user impls
// with custom semantics (e.g. an HTTP wrapper that treats body errors
// as failure) may have all three meaningful in any state.

trait tryable[V, C] =
  fn is_err(this tryable[V, C])    bool = virtual
  fn ok_value(this tryable[V, C])  V    = virtual
  fn err_value(this tryable[V, C]) C    = virtual
